Falsifies `capability_table::the_count_is_the_slots`.

The defect: `empty`, the one door that frees a slot, sets the slot's free bit and
does not decrement `used`. There is no separate call to forget,
because both freeing paths go through `empty`, so the mistake is at the door
itself. It is still the plausible one: `empty` now keeps two records (the free-slot
words and the count), and updating one of a pair is how a pair drifts.

The cost is not authority: every capability really is gone and the words say so,
so `insert` reuses the slot correctly. What breaks is the *record*. `used` stays
permanently above the truth, the high-water mark with it, and the number a boot
prints as `capability slots: N of M at peak` becomes an overstatement that nothing
else in the tree can contradict.

Expected to fail: the second assertion, `assert!(is_count_right_at(&cs, position),
"delete_matching left the count behind")`, on any table state where the sweep
frees an occupied slot. The first passes, because `put` goes through `fill`, which
this edit does not touch.

diff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs
--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -424,7 +424,6 @@
         pub(super) fn empty(&mut self, slot: usize) -> Option<Capability<O>> {
             let capability = self.slots.get_mut(slot)?.take()?;
             self.free[slot / WORD_BITS] |= 1u64 << (slot % WORD_BITS);
-            self.used -= 1;
             Some(capability)
         }
 
